  {
    "id": "6a68e426066bdd8b5a7d2424",
    "title": "Together, We’re Building a More Connected Future for Families",
    "slug": "together-we-re-building-a-more-connected-future-for-families",
    "excerpt": "CAPS & Maple City is officially operating as one organization, across five sites and a mobile unit, bringing together health care, family support, child abuse prevention education, and child advocacy services. By joining together, we can make it easier for children and families to access the care and support they need—often without having to navigate multiple organizations or travel across the county. A family receiving support after a difficult experience can be connected to health care. A...",
    "content": "CAPS & Maple City is officially operating as one organization, across five sites and a mobile unit, bringing together health care, family support, child abuse prevention education, and child advocacy services. By joining together, we can make it easier for children and families to access the care and support they need—often without having to navigate multiple organizations or travel across the county. A family receiving support after a difficult experience can be connected to health care. A patient visiting for a medical appointment may also be introduced to family support or prevention services. Through our mobile clinic, we can bring care closer to where people live, learn, work, and gather. At the heart of this work is one shared belief: every child and family in our community deserves the opportunity to be healthy, safe, and thriving. Community Support Made This Possible We are deeply grateful to the donors, partners, volunteers, board members, staff, and community leaders who have supported this journey. The Community Foundation of Elkhart County provided a generous $500,000 grant to help offset initial merger-related expenses and support the early work of bringing our organizations together. This investment is helping build the foundation for a stronger and more coordinated system of comprehensive care. “Together, we are building something greater than the sum of its parts,” said Rebecca Shetler Fast, CEO of CAPS & Maple City. “We are inspired by what lies ahead and remain committed to creating a seamless network of care that strengthens children, empowers families, and transforms lives across our community.” Pete McCown, President of the Community Foundation of Elkhart County, shared, “Strong communities are built when trusted organizations collaborate in ways that make services more accessible and effective. This merger reflects a commitment to stewardship, innovation, and long-term positive impact for families across our county.” Looking Ahead—Together The name CAPS & Maple City reflects both the trusted legacies that brought us here and the shared future we are building. As one organization, we remain deeply committed to the patients, clients, children, and families we serve. We are also committed to being thoughtful stewards of the resources our community entrusts to us. With the continued partnership of our supporters, we can reduce gaps in services, reach more families, and create a system of care that is more connected, more accessible, and easier to navigate. Thank you for believing in this work and for helping us build a healthier, safer community where children and families can thrive.",
