  {
    "id": "686d592cf04dc635589af1bb",
    "title": "What Is a CASA Volunteer? Giving Children a Voice When They Need It Most",
    "slug": "what-is-a-casa-volunteer-giving-children-a-voice-when-they-need-it-most",
    "excerpt": "...",
    "content": "When life gets messy for children caught in the chaos of the legal system, it can feel like the world is spinning too fast for them to keep up. This is where a CASA (Court Appointed Special Advocates) volunteer step in, as a consistent guiding presence for kids who need someone on their side. But what exactly is a CASA volunteer, and why is their role so important? Let’s take a deeper look at the role of a CASA volunteer. CASA Volunteers: The Voice for Children  CASA (Court Appointed Special Advocate) volunteers step in during a child’s toughest moments, becoming a vital helping hand. They ensure each child’s emotional, physical, and educational needs are met, providing far more than routine check-ins. They become the child’s voice in court and beyond. CASA volunteers offer stability and trust. They are a consistent adult presence a child can rely on, advocating for what is in the best interest of the child. A Lifeline for Stability  Here’s how Shannon McNett Silcox, a CASA volunteer, describes the impact: “CASA seemed like the perfect fit for me, to be able to be that voice for a child that maybe doesn’t have a voice, and to be able to stand up for them when they are afraid.” The quote beautifully captures the heart of what CASA volunteers do, stepping in when children are at their most vulnerable state and standing up for what the child’s needs and desires are. What Does a CASA Volunteer Do?  CASA volunteers work to ensure a child’s well-being. Here are a few ways CASA volunteers help:\n Building Relationships: Volunteers spend time getting to know the child to understand their needs, desires, and goals. In turn, developing trust along the way. Monitoring Well-Being: CASA volunteers ensure children are receiving proper services like healthcare and education. Advocating in Court: The most important role of a CASA volunteer is to speak up in court, providing insights into what is truly in the child’s best interest. \nA CASA volunteer’s testimony often becomes the judge’s compass for making decisions that could change the child’s future. Without CASA volunteers, children may not have anyone to represent their unique experiences or speak to their specific needs. Interested in learning more? https://capselkhart.org/casa-program",
    "published": "2024-11-01T17:22:48Z",
    "image": "wix:image://v1/e2a54c_d1eec9d32c054e0199bc29e3cafc78cc~mv2.png/e2a54c_d1eec9d32c054e0199bc29e3cafc78cc~mv2.png#originWidth=1024&originHeight=1024",
