  {
    "id": "6a2adf4c9095d1fb2e824aed",
    "title": "A New Chapter for Children and Families",
    "slug": "a-new-chapter-for-children-and-families",
    "excerpt": "Families rarely experience challenges in isolation. A child's safety, a family's health needs, financial stress, barriers to access, and family stability are all connected. By bringing child abuse prevention, family support and parenting education alongside primary, dental, and behavioral health care together, CAPS & Maple City is building a more coordinated and compassionate system of care in our community. Together we now provide more than 15 unique services and supports, integrating...",
    "content": "Families rarely experience challenges in isolation. A child's safety, a family's health needs, financial stress, barriers to access, and family stability are all connected. By bringing child abuse prevention, family support and parenting education alongside primary, dental, and behavioral health care together, CAPS & Maple City is building a more coordinated and compassionate system of care in our community. Together we now provide more than 15 unique services and supports, integrating clinical care and community services. Children and families thrive when they are safe, healthy, supported, and treated with dignity. This coordinated model of care is both practical and deeply personal. When a child and family comes to our doors after possible abuse or trauma, they are often facing one of the hardest moments of their lives. In that moment, every additional barrier matters. Having to travel to another city or navigate another system for medical care, behavioral health support, or follow-up services can add stress to an already overwhelming experience. As we work to integrate CAPS & Maple City, we are building a system where children and families can receive compassionate, trauma-informed care in one connected environment. A child may be able to receive a forensic interview, a medical evaluation, and connection to mental health support through the same coordinated team. A parent may be connected to health care, behavioral health, parenting support, or other resources without having to navigate multiple systems alone. Lizzy Diaz, SANE (Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner) Coordinator and a SANE Nurse, has shared “How we are meeting people where they are — how we choose to see them not just as a patient, but as a person who deserves dignity and respect, matters. We become not just a part of their medical journey, but a part of their healing.” Lizzy Diaz, SANE Coordinator and Nurse, highlights the special care put into a SANE Exam Room. That is the heart of our work: seeing the whole person and the whole family. The challenges families face are often connected, and the support available to them should be connected too. By providing a suite of both clinical and community service programming, CAPS & Maple City can help close gaps, strengthen protective factors, and support families earlier and more holistically. Our vision remains clear: to help build a community where children are safer, families are stronger, and health and well-being are within reach. With the support of donors, volunteers, partners, and neighbors, CAPS & Maple City is creating a healthier, safer community where children and families can thrive.",
    "published": "2026-06-11T16:44:05Z",
    "image": "wix:image://v1/57b283_7f072fd1e8334f128ac930b88e0ef6f0~mv2.jpg/57b283_7f072fd1e8334f128ac930b88e0ef6f0~mv2.jpg#originWidth=6048&originHeight=4032",
    "featured": false,
    "category": NaN,
    "tags": "[]"
  },
  {
    "id": "690262a1b041992f966a50aa",
    "title": "Why I Stand with CAPS",
    "slug": "why-i-stand-with-caps",
    "excerpt": "A Q&A with former detective and current CAPS board chair- Brook Germann Q: Why did you get involved with CAPS? A: I worked with CAPS while investigating abuse cases as a detective. I saw how they treated kids—with care, patience, and respect. After speaking at a CAPS event, I was invited to join the board. I said yes right away. I knew this was work worth standing behind. Q: What does this work mean to you, personally? A: I think about my kids. My grandkids. I want them to grow up in a place...",
    "content": "A Q&A with former detective and current CAPS board chair- Brook Germann Q: Why did you get involved with CAPS? A: I worked with CAPS while investigating abuse cases as a detective. I saw how they treated kids—with care, patience, and respect. After speaking at a CAPS event, I was invited to join the board. I said yes right away. I knew this was work worth standing behind. Q: What does this work mean to you, personally? A: I think about my kids. My grandkids. I want them to grow up in a place where they feel safe. That’s what CAPS is building—a safer future, one child at a time. It means everything to me. Q: What gives you hope? A: CAPS doesn’t treat families like numbers. They focus on each child, each story, each moment. And when you help one child, it spreads. Families share resources, neighbors step in, cycles get broken. That’s how change happens. Q: Why should others get involved? A: Because this is our community. CAPS shows up for kids in crisis every day. They need us—and we need them. When we support families, we build a stronger, safer community for all of us. No child should have to wait to be heard. Your support helps make sure they don’t.",
    "published": "2025-10-31T15:31:08Z",
    "image": "wix:image://v1/57b283_2969cc0137074148ab5e0d89e6d2e065~mv2.jpg/57b283_2969cc0137074148ab5e0d89e6d2e065~mv2.jpg#originWidth=5666&originHeight=3782",
